WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.010
A proof of a limit is also a set of instructions.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.460
To reach the limit exactly, nothing in the proof

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:07.350
may be wasted.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.760
Looking at where the proof is tight tells you how the

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.470
best arrangement must be built.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.240
Searching for the best arrangement by trial can take

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:17.250
a long time.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.240
Letting the proof guide you is faster, and it shows

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.210
whether the limit can be reached at all.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.540
Counters are placed on a four by four board,

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.290
at most one per square.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.060
Each row may contain at most two counters,

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.130
and each column at most two.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.250
What is the largest number of counters?

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.830
Pause and find the limit first.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:43.130
There are four rows, and each holds at

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:44.140
most two counters.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.460
So there are at most eight counters.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:47.980
That is the limit.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.800
To reach eight, every row must be completely full,

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.370
with exactly two counters.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.560
And the counters must be spread so that no column gets

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:57.370
more than two.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.120
Putting them all in the first two columns would break

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:00.950
the column rule.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:02.810
So share the columns.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.590
Rows one and two use columns one and two.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.630
Rows three and four use columns three and four.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.810
Every row has two, and every column has two.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:13.010
Eight is reached.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:16.620
Always check the finished arrangement against every

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.470
rule, not only the total.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.540
An arrangement can have the right number and still break

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:22.290
a rule somewhere.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.250
Here every row, every column and every square passes.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.270
Prove the limit.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.730
Ask what must be full to reach it.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:33.510
Build the arrangement, then check every rule.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.160
Notebook challenge: on a three by three board with at

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:40.340
most one counter per row and per column, what is the

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:43.110
largest number of counters, and where could they go?
